Page Table Coherence between CPU/GPU
This is a home grown ARM64 CPU tightly coupled with GPU for HPC and provides CPU/GPU hw cache coherence but two separate MMUs. The goal of this project is to provide uniform address space between CPU/GPU.

My role is to scope out / drive Linux VM subsystem changes / hw changes required for unified address space implementation. In addition I am implementing asynchronous fault support for faults originating from GPU. This uses kernel worker threads that use the use_mm interface to switch in required user address space in kernel context and make the GPU fault look like a cpu fault. Other highlights include modifying PTE_DIRTY macros to lookup GPU PTEs in addition to CPU PTEs for LRU page eviction. This is to make sure pages accessed by GPU dont always get evicted by OS. set_pte_at and tlb_flush routines for ARM are also modified to do remote tlb shootdowns on the gpu. Working closely with hw to improve GPU TLB invalidate performance. To avoid routing every CPU TLB inval request to GPU borrowed an extra bit in ARM PTE to record if the PTE is being shadowed on the GPU. Profiling impact of CPU initiated GPU TLB invalidate on GPU threads and working with hw to decrease victim latency.


T40 Tegra Memory Consistency Models:
Investigating changes required in the SW stack to implement Weak consistency model between Cortex A15 ARM CPU and Kepler generation GPU that share DRAM and memory controller but L2 caches are not coherent. 

Efforts involve changing fences/flushes/invalidates in gpu page table setup code / graphics context setup code / compute surface accesses. Also profiling hotspots in hw implementations of L2 flush/invalidate and volatile memory implementation and identifying accidental store serializations.



CUDA Page Based Memory Coherence (Upcoming Maxwell Architecture)
The goal is to allow CUDA programs to pass pointers between CPU and GPU SMs in a cache coherent way without asking programmers to do explicit memory copies. Targetted for systems where we dont own the CPU. This uses faults to do page copies implicitly. 

My role is to drive/ scope out Linux kernel VM changes for page based DSM implementation along with identifing GPU hw changes to make this task optimal especially in improving GPU page fault latencies. Currently implementing proof of concepts / identifying memory access patterns for customer apps and doing perf estimates. So far I ve implemented MOESI in software to track page ownership between CPU(s)/GPU(s) and also implemented asynchronous page migrations using GPU's dma engines.

False sharing is a huge problem with this approach. Another team is investigating in parallel a weaker consistency model that uses explicit acquire/releases, I am helping them in a supporting role.


